I have the studyplan.dev reference (paddle hit offset mapped to -1..1, and "temporarily disable the paddle's collider after a hit, ensuring clean, single bounces" — the multi-collision timer). Good references for the paddle family.

References collected:
1. **Tunneling / swept AABB**: https://blog.hamaluik.ca/posts/swept-aabb-collision-using-minkowski-difference/ — discrete sampling lets fast objects tunnel; swept tests give earliest time of impact.
2. **Swept AABB + min-axis resolution**: https://noonat.github.io/intersect/ — AABB vs AABB resolves along the smallest overlap axis; swept AABB for moving objects.
3. **Ghost collisions (seam)**: https://briansemrau.github.io/dealing-with-ghost-collisions/ — tile seams produce conflicting contact normals; solution = merge geometry / one normal.
4. **Paddle angle + multi-collision**: https://www.studyplan.dev/sdl2/sdl2-breakout-paddle-physics — map hit position to -1..1, disable collider after hit to avoid repeated collisions.
5. **Breakout angle (dead zone)**: https://gamedev.stackexchange.com/questions/... "Breakout angle calculation" — bounce 90° at center, 45° at edges (so vx=0 at exact center is the degenerate case to avoid).
6. **Circle-AABB closest point**: https://learnopengl.com/In-Practice/2D-Game/Collisions/Collision-detection — AABB-circle collision detection for breakout.

